diff --git a/docs/moe-cache.md b/docs/moe-cache.md
index 7491a2a..c3ebf1b 100644
--- a/docs/moe-cache.md
+++ b/docs/moe-cache.md
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@ When the experts of MoE layers stay in host memory (`-cmoe`, `-ncmoe`, `-ot`), t
 |---|---|
 | `--moe-cache-mib N` | cache size in MiB, split among the GPUs like the layers (default: 0, disabled) |
 | `--moe-cache-cpu-share F` | share of the cache misses computed on the CPU from the host experts instead of uploaded, once the cache is full (default: 0) |
+| `--moe-cache-policy P` | eviction policy: `classlru` (default) or `next_use`, see below |
 
 The cache hits run on the device. With `--moe-cache-cpu-share F` the CPU computes a share `F` of the misses while the device computes the hits and uploads the other misses into the cache. This is faster when the CPU reads the host experts faster than the device can upload them, for example with fast RAM, a PCIe 4 link or experts that are not in pinned memory. The host experts are pinned with `-lm none`, `-lm mlock` and `-lm dio`, unless `--no-host`, but not when they are mmap'd (the default). The device uploads pinned experts at the full speed of the link, so the CPU share often does not help then. Until the cache is full every miss is uploaded, so the cache fills as fast as without the CPU share. The best share depends on the machine, the model and the cache size.
 
@@ -24,3 +25,14 @@ From these runs it fits the upload and CPU speeds of the machine, then replays t
 - **Server command:** keep your own arguments (`-t`, `-c`, expert placement, load mode); the speeds and the free device memory depend on them.
 - **`--max-mib N`:** tunes a smaller cache than what fits on the device. The runs in `--out` are reused while the command stays the same, so trying another value is instant.
 - **`--markdown`:** prints Markdown tables to paste in a PR.
+
+## Eviction policy
+
+The cache keeps the experts of all its layers in one pool of slots. When it is full, a miss evicts one expert:
+
+- `classlru` (default): the least recently used expert of a frequency class, scaled by the class (ClassLRU).
+- `next_use`: the expert expected to be used last. Decode visits the cached layers in a fixed order, so an expert of layer L can next be used when that order reaches L again: `d` layer calls from now. It is used then with probability `p`, else one cycle later, which gives `d + n_layers*(1/p - 1)` layer calls. `p` is a per-expert reuse estimate that decays at every visit of its layer and grows at every use. The experts of the current layer are evicted last.
+
+Both policies only decide which expert leaves the cache, never which experts run, so the outputs do not change. `test-llama-archs` checks that the logits are bit-identical across cache sizes and policies.
+
+On routing recorded from DeepSeek-V4-Flash UD-IQ2_M (85 GiB, NVIDIA L4 24 GB, 1 to 8 parallel requests), replayed with `moe-cache-tune.py`, `next_use` had 4% to 9% fewer misses than `classlru` at 3 to 12 GiB of cache. The replay matched the measured `classlru` hit rate within 1 point. `moe-cache-tune.py` replays the policy given with `--moe-cache-policy` in the server command.
